# 0064-main-thread-never-walks-big-folders.patch -- no button, alert action or refresh walks or deletes a folder of thousands of files on the main thread; the foreground observer returns at once; the log page is bounded.
# Applies after 0063 (the tree with 0010..0063 applied). UNTESTED ON DEVICE: compiled (dotnet build, ios-arm64: rc 0, no error, the same 45 warnings as the tree without it) and the new classes run in a host harness
# (a net console program built from the real source text of GameAppPerf.cs and GameAppUi.cs, 70 checks); no iPhone run. Measured and assumed are told apart below.
#
# WHY (measured on the owner's iPhone, 2026-11-20, three crash reports of a 22,000-shader build): EXC_CRASH SIGKILL 0x8BADF00D, "scene-update watchdog transgression ... exhausted real (wall clock) time allowance of 10.00 seconds".
#   Reports 1 and 2: the MAIN thread in -[UIApplication _sendWillEnterForegroundCallbacks] -> our WillEnterForeground observer -> lstat. Report 3: the main thread in lstat from managed code inside -[UIButton _sendActionsForEvents]
#   -> -[UIControl sendAction:to:...]: a BUTTON HANDLER (twelve managed frames deep) walking files for over 10 s. The walk was the storage bar's FolderBytes (every refresh measured five folders, and every tab / game-sheet / selection
#   handler refreshes): Documents/shadercache (15,000+ files), and on the owner's phone after the cache and the logs were cleared Documents/shaders/<id>.work (the shader build's resume folder: 27,144 files, 6.2 GB, about 50,000 for
#   the 42,848 shaders of the game), still walked at every refresh: the app's own "first screen" lines were 586 ms (empty install) / 3,337 (9k cached shaders) / 5,090 (15k) / 3,892 / 2,278 (cache cleared, .work present) / 2,018.
#   About 0.1 to 0.3 ms a file on the phone (estimates from those lines, not a separate measurement). Deleting the logs changed nothing: the log screen is not the main cost (it is bounded anyway, below).
#
#   * GameAppPerf.cs (new; no UIKit, compiled as it is by the host harness; build-app.sh picks it up with the other GameApp*.cs):
#       FolderWalk.Bytes    the size of a folder through FileSystemEnumerable (one readdir and one stat a file, no path string and no FileInfo a file); an overload takes a filter on the top-level names (the packs of
#                           Documents/shaders apart from the .work folders). Worker threads only. Same totals as the old FolderBytes (checked on 60,000 files).
#       FolderSizes         the cache the screens read: last known size per folder (-1 = not known yet), measured by ONE Task.Run at a time (single flight: 16 threads asking 8,000 times = one walk of each folder), at least
#                           30 s between two walks of a folder, 60 s for the heavy ones (extract, shaders, shadercache) while a build runs, 5 minutes for a SLOW one (the .work folders: measured rarely, by a build's end or a delete,
#                           not by the clock), invalidated by the events that change a size (a build ends, a delete, 'Clear', an export, a removed export): measured again at once, except a heavy folder while a build runs; fifty
#                           invalidations in a row are one walk; an invalidation during a walk is not lost. NOTHING is measured while a delete runs (hold). The last value stays shown. One 'a value arrived' post to the main
#                           thread for several arrivals, which draws the storage bars, the shader cache's label and the open game sheet again, nothing else. Each walk's time goes to a hook (the launch-profile lines).
#       BackgroundDelete    recursive deletes on ONE worker thread, in order, `Busy` from the Enqueue until the job's `done` ran on the main thread: the reusable helper (GameAppViewController.DeleteFolderInBackground).
#       Coalescer           'run this once on a later turn of the run loop, however often it is asked, and not twice within N ms' (RefreshScreen: 250 ms; the log page's redraw: 400 ms).
#       Bg                  'work on a worker thread, the answer on the main thread' (the saves and the mods).
#       LaunchProfile       the `launch-profile: <part> <ms>` lines: in the first 90 s every part over 50 ms, afterwards a part over 250 ms and at most once per part every 10 s.
#       UiLogModel          the lines the log page holds (see GameAppLibrary.cs).
#   * GameAppMain.cs:
#       OnWillEnterForeground   the observer does nothing synchronously: BeginInvokeOnMainThread posts RefreshScreen, RefreshCloud(null) (its Keychain read of the NAS password and its file reads too) and a size request.
#       RefreshScreen           now a Coalescer request (next run-loop turn, once per 250 ms however many times it is asked: every tab, selection and sheet handler goes through it); RefreshScreenNow is the body, unchanged,
#                               and ViewDidLoad calls it (the first frame has the shelf in it). By reading the code RefreshScreen is NOT called by the 1 s tailer, the 1 s ticker or the progress lines.
#       CurrentStorageSegments  returns at once: the dumps' sizes (one short listing of Documents) and the free space now, the folders as last measured (0 until known; Documents/shaders = the packs + the .work folders +
#                               the shader cache, three tracked folders), then asks FolderSizes; the bars are drawn again when the numbers are in. RefreshCacheSize: the label from the cache ('Shader cache: …').
#       DeleteFolderInBackground(path, done)   REUSABLE: the folder is deleted on the delete worker (one at a time, in order), no size is measured meanwhile, the sizes are measured again when it is done, `done(error or null)` on
#                               the main thread. Clear shader cache (Settings, both screens) uses it: 'Shader cache: clearing…', then the same log line as before. The pause/cancel work can call it for a cancelled build's .work.
#       ConfirmDelete / PerformDelete / PlanBytes   the 'Frees ...' sizes on a worker thread; the deletion (files, then folders: extract/, shaders/<id>, shaders/<id>.work, ...) on the delete worker, same log lines in the same
#                               order; a second question and BeginRun are refused while one runs ('a delete is still going: try again in a moment').
#       ConfirmExport           the four walks of the alert's sizes (framework, extract/flat, shaders/<id>, the app bundle) on a worker thread; the alert comes up when they are in (ShowExportChoice, body unchanged).
#       AutoCopySaves           the zip of the save before a boot (Boot button) on a worker thread; the boot starts when it is done, so the game never starts before its save is safe.
#       Timed / NoteTime        launch-profile lines for GatherFacts, RefreshScreen and each part of it (keys, settings, storage, icons, cloud icons, selection, games, build tab, player, pointer), and in ViewDidLoad
#                               PrepareLog, SweepLeftoverParts, LoadCloud, LoadBuildQueue, PreparePlayerDocuments, BuildShelf, BuildCloudPage, BuildKeysPage, BuildLogsPage (the log page), BuildSettingsPage, BuildAppsPage,
#                               BuildQueuePage, BuildBuildTab, BuildTabBar, ShowTab(0), ReportInterruptedRunIfAny, RefreshGames, and every storage measurement ('launch-profile: storage/shaders-work 1830').
#       Finish, StartExport end, the 1 s ticker   invalidate (all / Exports) or ask (cheap when nothing is due).
#   * GameAppLibrary.cs: Append is linear in the text (it cut the rest off again at every line: 1,190 ms for a 594 KB block of 20,000 lines on the host, 0.8 ms now; the real per-tick batches are tens of lines, so this is a guard);
#       the lines go to UiLogModel (not a List of 6,000); the page is redrawn at most every 400 ms instead of rebuilt at every append (and timed: 'log rebuild'). The FILES are written first and are unchanged. An exported player's
#       title screen reads the save folder (Current, List) on a worker thread, at most every 30 s, instead of at every refresh. A removed export invalidates Exports.
#   * GameAppShelf.cs: the Clear alert as above; the game sheet's Build and Shaders lines show '…' and are filled when FolderSizes has them (shaders/<id> is its own tracked folder, measured when the sheet opens).
#   * GameAppSavesUi.cs: the saves page (Current = a walk of the save folder, the copies' listing) is filled from a worker thread; 'Save a copy now' (a zip of the save), 'Replace' (the copy of the save it replaces, the staging
#       delete, the swap, the delete of the old folder) and the JKSV export run on a worker thread; one at a time.
#   * GameAppModsUi.cs: the mods page (GameMods.List walks every mod's RomFS tree: also at every switch) is filled from a worker thread; Delete (a mod's folder) and both imports (the picked zip's copy and unpacking, a folder's
#       walk and copy; the folder's security scope is kept until it is done) run on a worker thread; one at a time.
#
# MEASURED (host harness, Apple silicon Mac, warm file cache, .NET 11, 60,000 files of 64..963 bytes in 600 folders, the same files under shadercache and under shaders/<id>.work):
#       old FolderBytes of the 60,000 files 154-247 ms (median ~160-180) on the MAIN thread: CurrentStorageSegments did it five times over (173-188 ms), Documents/shaders with the .work folder 171 ms. New FolderWalk 132-173 ms on a
#       worker. The main thread's cost of the same bars: the first call 0.1-1.0 ms (it starts the worker), then 0.05-1.4 us a call. The packs of Documents/shaders alone: 0.6 ms; the .work folder: 146 ms, on the worker, once.
#       Directory.Delete(recursive) of the 60,000 files: 3,203 ms (the old handler, on the main thread; the phone is slower per file: seconds to tens of seconds); enqueued on the delete worker: 0.4 ms on the calling thread.
#       Single flight / interval / invalidation / hold-while-deleting / slow key / coalescer / delete queue (in order, one at a time, a failing job reports and the next one runs): 70 of 70 checks.
#       The log page, on the owner's real 1,944-line log: 1,012 rows before, 246 now (766 'unity compute source' lines left out of the page, 926 counter/step lines merged in place; the rows are exactly the old timeline's rows
#       without the compute lines). One 22,000-shader build as the engine writes it (2,156 lines): 416 rows before, 46 now. GameAppUi.Timeline from 1,944 lines 2.2 ms, from the model's 252 lines 0.33 ms; the old cap of 6,000
#       lines, rebuilt at every append while the page showed: 6.9 ms. UiLogModel.Add 0.4 us a line.
# NOT MEASURED / ASSUMED: every number above is a Mac's; the phone's file system is 30 times slower or more per file (see WHY), so a 60,000-file walk or delete there is seconds to tens of seconds: the point of the patch is that none of
#       it is on the main thread. That the foreground return and the button handlers no longer trip the watchdog is argued from the three stacks, not demonstrated. The UIKit cost of the log page, the bars and the saves / mods pages
#       is not measured. RefreshScreen's remaining work is proportional to the number of GAMES (a few listings of Documents, one cover lookup a game); GatherFacts' Keychain read and address-space read are untouched.
# LEFT ALONE (all shallow, bounded by the number of games or a few files): SweepLeftoverParts at launch (Documents, Exports/<folder>, SaveBackups/<folder>: one level each), Dumps / DocumentNames / DumpBytes / GatherFacts' listings
#       (one level of Documents) and InstalledShaderPacks (one File.Exists per entry of shaders/), AppsSummary / FillApps / GameAppUi.AllRomfs (Exports, one level, a folder a game), RemoveEmptyGameFolder (one non-recursive delete of an
#       empty folder), GameAppDiag.Rotate / ShareFiles / SaveMetric (.diag: six sessions, metrickit 40 files), the lifecycle observers of GameAppDiag (MemLine = task_info + statfs, one line written), GameAppHost.Background /
#       Active (dlsym calls into the host), CopyIn's File.Move (a rename inside the container; its File.Copy fallback is one big file, not a walk: patch 0060's), Delete of a downloaded dump (one unlink). Already on a worker:
#       Export / PlaceRomfs, ScanFolderForContent, RefreshCloud's folder listing, CopyWithProgress and Nas.Download, ShareGameLogs / LogWindow.BuildZip, FetchMissingIcons, GameWarm.Run.
